course: chemistry
lesson: 1
title: What is everything made of?
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Cut a slice of cheese in half, then in half again, and again: the pieces get smaller, but you cannot go on forever.
explain: In the end everything (cheese, water, air, your own hand) is built from tiny pieces called atoms, far too small to see even with a school microscope.
explain: Atoms and the particles they make are never still: they jiggle, bump and drift, which is why Mum's perfume reaches your nose from across the room without anybody waving it.
explain: Atoms are not destroyed when ice melts or a smell spreads; the same atoms just move about and change partners.
explain: Nobody has seen an atom with bare eyes, but careful experiments over two hundred years showed they are real, and special microscopes can now map single atoms.
fact: Everything around us is made of tiny particles called atoms.
fact: A single hair is hundreds of thousands of atoms wide.
fact: Even one pencil dot holds more atoms than the number of people who have ever lived.
fact: Pencil "lead" is graphite, a kind of carbon, mixed with clay; it contains no lead metal.
fact: Particles in air and water are always moving and bumping, so smells and colours spread out on their own.
fact: Particles move faster when they are warmer, so things spread faster in heat and slower in cold.
fact: A salt grain is a tiny cube because salt's particles stack in a cube pattern; a sugar grain has flat faces too, but a different shape.
fact: Most atoms in your body, such as carbon, oxygen and iron, were made inside stars long ago; hydrogen is even older, from almost the beginning of the universe.
fact: An atom is made of smaller parts: protons and neutrons in a tiny middle called the nucleus, and electrons around it.
fact: Electrons have a negative charge and protons a positive one; opposite charges attract, and charges that are the same push each other apart.
fact: Your hand does not sink into a table because the electrons in your hand and in the table push each other away.
fact: Rubbing a balloon on a jumper moves electrons onto the balloon, so it becomes charged and can cling to a wall; on a damp day the water in the air lets the charge leak away, so the trick works badly.
fact: Your body is built from the atoms in the food you eat, the water you drink and the air you breathe.
fact: When ice melts, the same water molecules (a molecule = a small group of atoms joined together) are there before and after; melted ice weighs the same as the ice did.
fact: Ancient Greek thinkers guessed at four elements (earth, air, fire and water); Democritus guessed at uncuttable bits he called atoms («atom» comes from the Greek for «cannot be cut»), but only later experiments showed atoms are real. Today we know of over a hundred kinds of atom.
exercise: You cut a slice of cheese in half again and again. What would you reach in the end, if you had a perfect knife? | Atoms, tiny pieces that everything is made of | Smaller and smaller cheese, forever | Nothing at all, the cheese would vanish | Tiny drops of milk and water
exercise: About how many atoms wide is a single hair? | Hundreds of thousands | About ten, at the most | Roughly a hundred or so | Exactly one thousand and one
exercise: What is the grey dot from a pencil made of? | Carbon atoms from graphite | Tiny bits of soft grey lead metal | Grey paint rubbed off the wood | Burnt paper dust from the page
exercise: How does Mum's perfume reach you across the room? | Its particles drift and bump through the air to your nose | Your nose pulls the smell towards it | The perfume bottle shoots the smell straight at you | Smells only travel if a window is open
exercise: Why does a drop of food colouring spread through still water with no stirring? | The water particles keep jiggling and knock the colour about | The colour is heavier than water and sinks | The glass pushes the colour outwards from the sides | Food colouring is alive and swims through the water
exercise: Why are salt grains under a magnifier little cubes? | Salt's particles stack in a cube-shaped pattern | Factories cut each grain into a cube | Salt grains get rubbed square inside the shaker | All grains of every substance are cubes
exercise: What does "you are made of stardust" really mean? | Most of your atoms were made inside stars long ago | Bits of the stars fall into your food | You glow faintly in the dark like a star | Your body makes new atoms from sunlight
exercise: What are the tiny parts inside an atom? | Protons, neutrons and electrons | Tiny cells, blood and little bones | Molecules, crystals and small grains | The four elements: fire, water and air
exercise: Two magnets push each other away. What kind of push holds atoms together? | Electric pushes and pulls between charged parts | Gravity, the very same pull that the Earth has on us | Glue made from tiny threads | Air pressure squeezing them together
exercise: Why does a rubbed balloon stick to the wall? | Rubbing gave it extra electrons, and the charge pulls on the wall | Rubbing makes the rubber sticky, like the back of a piece of tape | The jumper leaves glue on the balloon | The balloon gets warm and melts onto the wall
exercise: An ice cube melts in your hand. What happens to its water molecules? | The same molecules start sliding past each other | They are destroyed and new ones made | They turn into air molecules | Half of them disappear into your hand
exercise: Who guessed long ago that things are made of tiny uncuttable bits? | Democritus, an ancient Greek thinker | Isaac Newton, after seeing an apple fall | A Roman emperor who loved gold and jewels | A modern scientist using a big computer
exercise: What does the word "atom" mean in Greek? | Cannot be cut | Very, very small | Made of stardust | Building block
exercise: Why can you smell food cooking upstairs but not a cold sandwich? | Hot food sends off faster particles that spread more | Cold food has no particles at all, so there is nothing to smell | Sandwiches are wrapped in invisible plastic | Smells only rise when they are cold
test: Your mate says: "I chewed my pencil, I'm gonna get lead poisoning!" What do you tell him? | Pencil lead is graphite and clay, there is no lead metal in it | He's right, he should go to hospital at once | Only the red pencils still contain real lead, so check the colour | Pencils have lead only in the rubber at the end
test: What would happen if you put food colouring in ice-cold water instead of hot water? | It would spread much more slowly | It would not spread at all, ever | It would spread faster than in hot water | It would freeze into a coloured lump at once
test: "If atoms are mostly empty space, why can't I walk through the wall, huh?" Best answer? | Electrons in you and the wall push each other away hard | Walls are the only thing not made of atoms | You could, if you ran at it fast enough and slipped between them | The empty space is filled with glue
test: In a cartoon, an ice cube melts and the puddle weighs much less than the ice. What's wrong? | The puddle weighs the same, the molecules are all still there | Nothing, water is always lighter than ice, because ice is packed tighter | The puddle should weigh twice as much | Melted water weighs nothing at all
test: Gran says: "Everything is made of earth, air, fire and water, love." What is the truth? | That was an old Greek guess; we now know of over a hundred kinds of atom | She is right, scientists still use those four elements and no others today | There are exactly four atoms, one for each | Only living things are made of those four
test: "nobody's ever seen an atom so they're made up, obviously" Is that right? | No, experiments show they are real and special microscopes map them | Yes, atoms are only an idea in books | Yes, scientists invented them to explain magic | No, you can see each one with an ordinary magnifying glass from home
test: On a damp day the balloon trick keeps failing. Why? | Water in the air lets the charge leak away | Damp air is too heavy for the balloon to float in | Wet walls are too slippery to stick to | Balloons lose their electrons in the dark
test: Where were the atoms of your hand a hundred years ago? | Somewhere else, in food, air, soil, other living things | Nowhere, because your body made brand new atoms when you were born | In the same hand, waiting for you | Inside the Sun, which sent them down
test: Your sister says a pencil dot has "about a hundred atoms in it". Put her right. | It holds billions upon billions of them | It holds exactly one big atom | It has no atoms, only ink | She is right, about a hundred
test: Why does your hair stand up after you pull off a woolly hat? | Rubbing charged your hairs alike, so they push each other apart | The wool pulls each hair upward by gravity | Your hair gets cold and stiff, like grass on a frosty morning | The hat leaves sticky wool on each hair
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the constitution of ordinary matter? | All matter is composed of minute particles designated atoms | Matter may be subdivided indefinitely without limit | Matter consists of the four elements earth, air, fire and water | Only living matter is composed of particles
exam: It is given that a coloured substance disperses throughout still water in the absence of agitation. Identify the principal cause. | The continual random motion of the particles of the water | The superior weight of the colouring agent, which descends and spreads | Attraction exerted by the walls of the vessel | Currents generated by the observer's breath
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the composition of an atom. | It comprises a nucleus of protons and neutrons, surrounded by electrons | It comprises a solid indivisible sphere with no internal parts whatsoever | It comprises a multitude of smaller molecules | It comprises a single electron alone
exam: Ascertain the mass of the water obtained upon the complete melting of an ice cube whereof the mass is ten grams. | Ten grams, the quantity of matter being unaltered | Less than ten grams, owing to the loss of molecules | More than ten grams, owing to absorption of heat | Zero grams, liquids being without mass
